#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151932 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "5fe8dafe-b03c-54b6-a737-6572f59b97f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6995" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/1191de029d927394dcc587f9c432a2d7cf4cec136e6d6ebbe62799de7b4f9b3e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,1] \\times [0,1],$ and\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D |x-y| \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ |x-y|, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/d9ca0bfcfde5839033279cb09f8ccece7c59a5b8f8b24eaae3697fd5358dc6b3.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $|x-y|$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D |x-y| \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,\\backslash D.$ For example, $(x,y) = (1,0) \\in R\\,\\backslash\\, D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions II and III equal $|x-y|$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151933 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "80f011ae-c472-5dfa-8303-70396ecf51fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6996" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/7325a35720157e2331ade1b9abd042f90030361c1560dd3bf98de08cc495e4c4.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/d1457393a0b919a208da95e0b819b50a83847d2a5dfbb9c4c81c4c457a2cd467.png)\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n\n- $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n\n- $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "For this question, $R$ must contain every point of $D$. Extending the integrand by zero on $R\\setminus D$ then preserves the integral: the function equals the original integrand throughout $D$ and contributes zero outside it. The rectangles must therefore be checked for containment, as explicitly required in the prompt.\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/8e0d0d508d61352edf461f147cc561a527f6b3191e6c6b11d070eedf1e6572e3.png)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151934 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "fe8eb478-6830-55bc-84c6-668924daaefc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6997" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $x^2+y^2=9.$ If \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/77c124998f7dba480ae7a68547a72d35b86c3946e7efbf51072896051c9aeca6.png) \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-4,4] \\times [-4,4]$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,2]$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-3,3] \\times [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/3aab7a115fe72d3796be28c66f4ce9ae2c15dc56e09efd98e5ad9a45edebd704.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/676f189bafbe67e0d7c0562ae83921fe9ea27d0123ff81979cd64baf55d1dcc6.png)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f86e3899-8e2b-50c3-86c0-66c4ffe8f623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331250} {:answer/id #uuid "010e222e-228e-58a9-8d7c-9be146dba56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331251} {:answer/id #uuid "f2513807-eae9-58a0-b677-6a0299fb7a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331252} {:answer/id #uuid "95d1bcb9-311e-5b4a-9771-f8ab0cb83999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331253} {:answer/id #uuid "46ee0f1d-445d-53d9-b18f-a023bef243e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95d1bcb9-311e-5b4a-9771-f8ab0cb83999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331253} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0d50477-0e2e-5729-8b81-5cc6073d4a3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2998acef-0e56-5ee3-8025-29355342aad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-129658" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2542da32-68a0-53b6-942d-775aa8ee5e18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331257} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9717b4-bda6-5c16-a872-3c9e68626b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331258} {:answer/id #uuid "3259c574-de60-518d-a827-76ca7d1eede2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331259} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b65101-f209-53bc-8d2f-89f6870f9f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331260} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6991b4-a0e1-5ded-a9c6-13ebdc588d70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331261}] :answer-