at in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "881491ec-58d2-590b-b82a-17ce778f115d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151405} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c5ce70-0d54-55ec-8e04-3f4ed8c4debe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151406} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2d8a06-5188-58b3-a574-72be6221542c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151407} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9c22a4-be5d-51b9-973a-108340aac72e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151408} {:answer/id #uuid "7858a5f9-c6d7-5e62-8d5f-0b3f76c2d57b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6c5ce70-0d54-55ec-8e04-3f4ed8c4debe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151406} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ae59f8c-bdfd-5e73-9a42-9cfdc24c36e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ba1b157-d35e-5ebb-b99d-e367dc0feceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-80438" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/e8392135cf2ffbfd91d51fee015cc4ddc6778b890e40e1f63eae18b6927e85e8.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-6,6]$\n- ${∬}_{R}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n- ${∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,4]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b4ea34-20c6-59f6-9f96-7af7286e254c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331292} {:answer/id #uuid "d70435ac-0cb1-51ff-9927-23e5a373192d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331293} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fa6afe-d15d-51e8-b0d3-cbec03bd754b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331294} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6600cd-cc42-52cb-ae1d-aa65e55a2958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331295} {:answer/id #uuid "cff86dae-f973-5703-8666-822171e35fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cff86dae-f973-5703-8666-822171e35fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331296} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a3b41d-ccd8-5656-b35c-dd74bbb0706c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41942a74-5957-5b5b-8aba-bd38af9f8d4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80503" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/9d4a0a08513d817fe841f1e45e5d363d6e64241837dea71f38aef452f86b08a7.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[0,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8940604e-d5d7-51b1-bf55-202f74463393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331299} {:answer/id #uuid "5d47a83f-794a-5a9a-996e-389a39289ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331300} {:answer/id #uuid "5578d825-83d1-59ed-9be0-06614749d0cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331301} {:answer/id #uuid "966cb8c3-1c2c-5d45-be48-9bbcd486575f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331302} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5f4159-a261-5c71-8a06-965ea4517477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d47a83f-794a-5a9a-996e-389a39289ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331300} :answer-field/id #uuid "81609633-6fc6-5788-a39c-1839631bbfe4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6eb5a26d-0c9a-5754-8686-78fa12fef675" :question/math-academy-id "q-80540" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/506a298041a875549ec60c5cf99ebdbeb5ca4b5717037083fa6ca196fce27279.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ \\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/d2b5c55b15f594680e8f299899d690cd1d92baa1ebef31ce5c79af52afec631e.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{2x+y}{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function II. Since $D∖R$ is empty, the function $f(x,y)$ will be identical to zero for all points in $R,$ including $D.$ But $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ is not zero, for example, at $(x,y)=(0,2)\\in D.$\n\n- Functions I and III equals $\\frac{2x+y}{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7433f8ae-ee4d-5d84-af1b-23312a4f1801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331306} {:answer/id #uuid "67361ac1-5984-59ae-a801-e8f3b697b3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331307} {:answer/id #uuid "af54e9bb-7e45-56b8-bd65-32ad74d3ee05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331308} {:answer/id #uuid "6e17ad15-193e-543f-9dfb-3a2d65ef1c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331309} {:answer/id #uuid "aed8824a-9d2b-5f4b-b0c7-e408c7aef1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aed8824a-9d2b-5f4b-b0c7-e408c7aef1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331310} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e09ac83-a1f1-5415-9362-4d9556eb82a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d9add5b-8f17-5847-9dba-243391d78ce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-80566" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/d92fdb93f7564e1c205ab8603945eb6704c03db92c46130810e2b7cbce9a0e20.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- ${∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "906b8701-f8da-5d7f-aa3a-e7b0f0883fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331313} {:answer/id #uuid "2e10c23c-fc9a-5931-b776-c4515e3ad425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331314} {:answer/id #uuid "b273564a-78c8-5a5f-9468-8f9e90ac2357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331315} {:answer/id #uuid "5d5d1d58-7b5d-57e1-a7e1-ffb3f2214b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331316} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3f104d-1aaf-5dcb-9cfb-a843ae8a5977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e10c23c-fc9a-5931-b776-c4515e3ad425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331314} :answer-field/id #uuid "c651068d-6f24-5be6-a64b-162c10174c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92835db6-2edf-51dc-aeaa-39f18c91e825" :question/math-academy-id "q-80567" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{2}-4$ and the $x$-axis. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}3x-y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$\n- ${∬}_{R}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$\n- ${∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/a7af801fa9975dda6ce3b12b2cde2c328721cd5e0affd16f1a14ab26cfa21bdd.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}(3x-y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3b2b2a-54a5-5cb4-b3b1-c64f7064db44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/i