6bd62ebda425d182ae8d9e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7feb070b-d411-5942-b973-0540c9beb826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331327} {:answer/id #uuid "4780d85b-671e-5fb0-ac45-cf889371d904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331328} {:answer/id #uuid "86b7a658-e277-5168-a114-b66175e5b864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331329} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2e1653-01e5-5093-a302-af5d75dde458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331330} {:answer/id #uuid "787704ed-9c40-52b2-94a1-dcaf251fa4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "787704ed-9c40-52b2-94a1-dcaf251fa4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331331} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e0def1c-cf2e-5f57-970f-1d7ed1d0decb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da262874-2a96-5dfa-ad3e-eff09851e875" :question/math-academy-id "q-86899" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-|x|+2$ and $y=|x|.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/f3ffc82025f80c357c06e28fcb091c701d53d84821bebd9d2886cf4972b77028.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{5}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal ${x}^{5}-{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
